    "documentation": {
      "format": "markdown",
      "content": "**Scientific study:** [dataset-study-dry-bean on GitHub](https://github.com/FabioAguiar/dataset-study-dry-bean)  \n**Dataset source:** [Dry Bean — UCI Machine Learning Repository](https://archive.ics.uci.edu/dataset/602/dry+bean+dataset)  \n**Pinned scientific revision:** `e3e697c1b60f7b11f4ccb7e82447a6680bc2bfaf`  \n**Scientific reproduction run:** `repro-20260929T163341Z` — `reproduced_within_tolerance`\n\nThis dataset presents a **multiclass-classification** problem based on numerical morphology measurements extracted from images of dry bean grains. The objective is to distinguish seven nominal bean varieties from 16 measurements describing size, dimensions, elongation, convexity, compactness, roundness, and related shape characteristics.\n\nThe analysis is predictive rather than causal. Morphological associations describe statistical structure in the observed sample; they do not establish causal or agronomic effects.\n\n## Dataset overview\n\n| Item | Value |\n|---|---:|\n| Bean samples | 13,611 |\n| Source columns | 17 |\n| Predictive features | 16 numerical features |\n| Target | `Class` |\n| Target classes | 7 |\n| Problem type | Multiclass classification |\n| Selected model | HistGradientBoostingClassifier |\n| Selected feature policy | `all_features` |\n| Primary model-selection metric | Macro F1 |\n| Validation Macro F1 | 0.937881 |\n| Final-test Macro F1 | **0.941835** |\n| Final-test Balanced Accuracy | **0.939897** |\n\nEach row represents one dry bean grain described by numerical morphology measurements already extracted from images.\n\nThe seven target classes are:\n\n- `SEKER`\n- `BARBUNYA`\n- `BOMBAY`\n- `CALI`\n- `DERMASON`\n- `HOROZ`\n- `SIRA`\n\nThe target is nominal and unordered. There is no positive class and no binary decision threshold.\n\nThe predictive workflow starts after image feature extraction; it does not reproduce the original image-acquisition, segmentation, or measurement process.\n\n## Scientific lineage in Atlas\n\nThe Dry Bean dataset is represented in Atlas through distinct lineages that should not be interpreted as the same experiment.\n\n### Scientific Study\n\nThe scientific results documented on this page belong to the pinned Dataset Study revision:\n\n```text\ne3e697c1b60f7b11f4ccb7e82447a6680bc2bfaf\n```\n\nThis revision defines the scientific protocol, model-selection process, frozen configuration, final evaluation, diagnostics, limitations, and interpretation documented below.\n\n### Scientific Reproduction\n\nAtlas independently reproduced the pinned Scientific Study protocol in:\n\n```text\nrepro-20260929T163341Z\n```\n\nwith status:\n\n```text\nreproduced_within_tolerance\n```\n\nThe reproduction confirms that the scientific results can be recovered under the Atlas reproduction environment within the declared comparison tolerances.\n\nThe reproduction lineage remains separate from Atlas Native Training. Reproducing the scientific protocol does not convert the Scientific Study into a Native training run.\n\n### Atlas Native Release\n\nThe interactive Atlas inference experience uses the active Atlas Native release:\n\n```text\nrelease-20260818-002\n```\n\nThis is a separate training lineage with its own split and fixed-configuration training protocol.\n\nIts metrics are therefore **not directly comparable** with the Scientific Study metrics documented below, even when metric names are the same.\n\nScientific Study results should be interpreted as evidence about the pinned research protocol. Atlas Native results describe the independently trained model used by the Atlas inference runtime.\n\n## Analytical question\n\nThe analysis addresses two related questions:\n\n1. **How do the observed morphology profiles differ among the seven bean varieties?**\n2. **How effectively can those measurements classify held-out bean samples under the frozen evaluation protocol?**\n\nThe classifier uses the combined predictive information contained in the 16 numerical measurements and produces a probability distribution across all seven classes.\n\nA final predicted class is obtained by selecting the class with the largest score or probability.\n\nObserved relationships between a measurement and `Class` should not be interpreted as evidence that the measurement independently determines bean variety.\n\n## Feature and target contract\n\nThe model uses the following numerical predictors:\n\n```text\nArea\nPerimeter\nMajorAxisLength\nMinorAxisLength\nAspectRatio\nEccentricity\nConvexArea\nEquivDiameter\nExtent\nSolidity\nRoundness\nCompactness\nShapeFactor1\nShapeFactor2\nShapeFactor3\nShapeFactor4\n```\n\nThe target column is:\n\n```text\nClass\n```\n\n`Class` is never used as a predictive input.\n\nThe source provides no observation identifier. Exact equality between feature profiles therefore cannot establish that two records represent the same physical grain.\n\n## Data quality and preparation\n\nThe source contains 13,611 observations and 16 numerical candidate features.\n\nPreparation preserves the source shape exactly:\n\n| Check | Result |\n|---|---:|\n| Source rows | 13,611 |\n| Prepared rows | 13,611 |\n| Source columns | 17 |\n| Prepared columns | 17 |\n| Rows removed | 0 |\n| Generic imputation | None |\n| Automatic outlier deletion | None |\n| Predictive features retained | 16 of 16 |\n| Learned preprocessing during preparation | None |\n\nThe data is divided with a stratified **70% / 15% / 15%** split using a two-stage procedure:\n\n1. seed `42` separates the training partition from the temporary holdout;\n2. seed `43` separates the temporary holdout into validation and final-test partitions.\n\n| Partition | Rows | Purpose |\n|---|---:|---|\n| Train | 9,527 | Cross-validation, exploratory analysis, and model-family search |\n| Validation | 2,042 | Frozen candidate comparison and selection |\n| Final test | 2,042 | Final evaluation after selection decisions are frozen |\n\nThe split is frozen **before any distribution-aware or target-aware exploratory analysis**.\n\nExploratory distributions, feature-to-target associations, redundancy analyses, class profiles, and PCA results presented below are derived from the **training partition only (9,527 rows)** unless a section is explicitly identified as a source-level summary.\n\nIn the corrected canonical execution, the final-test partition does not participate in model-family search, feature-policy decisions, hyperparameter selection, or exploratory analysis.\n\nThe same seed-42 final-test partition had, however, already been evaluated once by a **superseded pre-correction execution**. For that reason, the current study does not describe the partition as never previously seen or never previously opened.\n\nRepeated feature profiles are preserved. Because the source has no observation identifier, repeated-profile evidence does not prove duplicate identity or leakage.\n\n## Class distribution\n\nThe following table describes class support in the **complete source dataset before splitting**.\n\n| Class | Rows | Share |\n|---|---:|---:|\n| `SEKER` | 2,027 | 14.89% |\n| `BARBUNYA` | 1,322 | 9.71% |\n| `BOMBAY` | 522 | 3.84% |\n| `CALI` | 1,630 | 11.98% |\n| `DERMASON` | 3,546 | 26.05% |\n| `HOROZ` | 1,928 | 14.17% |\n| `SIRA` | 2,636 | 19.37% |\n\n`DERMASON` is the most frequent class and `BOMBAY` the least frequent. The majority/minority support ratio is approximately `6.79`.\n\nThe following exploratory class-distribution figure is computed from the **training partition only**:\n\n![Dry Bean target class distribution](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/FabioAguiar/dataset-study-dry-bean/e3e697c1b60f7b11f4ccb7e82447a6680bc2bfaf/docs/images/target_class_distribution.png)\n\nBecause support differs across classes, overall accuracy alone is not sufficient. Macro-averaged metrics give every class equal importance.\n\n## Key exploratory findings\n\nAll exploratory findings in this section are derived from the training partition after the split has been frozen.\n\n### Morphology contains strong class-related structure\n\nSeveral morphology measurements show substantial univariate association with `Class`.\n\n![Univariate feature-to-target associations](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/FabioAguiar/dataset-study-dry-bean/e3e697c1b60f7b11f4ccb7e82447a6680bc2bfaf/docs/images/feature_target_association_ranking.png)\n\nThe associations indicate predictive signal but are descriptive rather than causal.\n\n### The feature space contains structural redundancy\n\nSeveral numerical variables are strongly related, and some are mathematically derived from other measurements.\n\n![Numerical feature correlation heatmap](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/FabioAguiar/dataset-study-dry-bean/e3e697c1b60f7b11f4ccb7e82447a6680bc2bfaf/docs/images/numerical_feature_correlation_heatmap.png)\n\nNine derived dependencies are numerically confirmed:\n\n```text\nAspectRatio\nEccentricity\nEquivDiameter\nSolidity\nRoundness\nCompactness\nShapeFactor1\nShapeFactor3\nShapeFactor4\n```\n\n`ShapeFactor2` is handled separately. Its audited source formula is not numerically confirmed at the configured tolerance, so its provenance remains `unresolved`.\n\nThis does not imply that the feature is invalid. Its predictive contribution is evaluated independently through controlled feature-retention experiments.\n\n### Reduced-dimensional views show both separation and overlap\n\n![Exploratory PCA class projection](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/FabioAguiar/dataset-study-dry-bean/e3e697c1b60f7b11f4ccb7e82447a6680bc2bfaf/docs/images/class_pca_projection.png)\n\nThe PCA projection is exploratory only. It shows meaningful class structure together with overlap among some varieties, but it is not itself a classifier and does not replace the full predictive feature space.\n\n![Standardized class profiles](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/FabioAguiar/dataset-study-dry-bean/e3e697c1b60f7b11f4ccb7e82447a6680bc2bfaf/docs/images/standardized_class_profiles.png)\n\n## Evaluation protocol\n\n**Macro F1** is the primary model-selection metric because every class should contribute equally despite unequal support.\n\nComplementary metrics include:\n\n- Balanced Accuracy;\n- Macro Recall;\n- Weighted F1;\n- overall Accuracy as contextual evidence;\n- per-class precision, recall, and F1;\n- multiclass Log Loss.\n\nCandidate-family search uses training-only five-fold stratified cross-validation:\n\n```text\nStratifiedKFold\nn_splits = 5\nshuffle = True\nrandom_state = 42\n```\n\nA candidate must exceed the Dummy validation Macro F1 by more than `0.02`.\n\nA practical validation tie is defined as a Macro-F1 difference no greater than `0.002`.\n\nWhen candidates fall inside that tolerance, predefined evidence is used to resolve the tie in the documented decision sequence, including Balanced Accuracy, worst-class recall, cross-validation stability, comparable Log Loss, simplicity, and stable model identity.\n\nThe final-test partition does not participate in selection.\n\n## Model selection\n\nThe study uses a two-stage selection process.\n\nFirst, four trainable model families are searched and ranked by training-only cross-validation. The two strongest families form the shortlist.\n\nSecond, the shortlisted families are evaluated under controlled feature-retention policies before final selection on the validation partition.\n\n### Candidate-family comparison\n\n| Model family | Search | Evaluated configurations | CV Macro F1 mean ± std | CV Balanced Accuracy | CV Weighted F1 | CV Log Loss ↓ | Outcome |\n|---|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|---|\n| HistGradientBoostingClassifier | RandomizedSearchCV | 8 of 64 | **0.937012 ± 0.003825** | **0.935424** | **0.924310** | 0.258114 | **Shortlisted** |\n| LogisticRegression | GridSearchCV | 4 of 4 | 0.935031 ± 0.000996 | 0.933826 | 0.922071 | **0.218538** | **Shortlisted** |\n| RandomForestClassifier | RandomizedSearchCV | 8 of 24 | 0.933343 ± 0.003635 | 0.931656 | 0.921274 | 0.342768 | Candidate |\n| DecisionTreeClassifier | GridSearchCV | 8 of 8 | 0.919904 ± 0.009698 | 0.920557 | 0.908862 | 0.662884 | Candidate |\n\nDecision Tree and Random Forest are not taken into the later validation-policy stage because they do not enter the CV-only shortlist.\n\n### Candidate search configuration and hyperparameters\n\n| Model | Search policy | Hyperparameters evaluated |\n|---|---|---|\n| Logistic Regression | GridSearchCV, 4/4 | `C={0.1,1.0}`; `class_weight={None,balanced}`; fixed `solver=lbfgs`, `max_iter=2000`, `random_state=42`; `StandardScaler` fitted within each fold |\n| Decision Tree | GridSearchCV, 8/8 | `max_depth={8,None}`; `min_samples_leaf={1,5}`; `class_weight={None,balanced}`; fixed `random_state=42` |\n| Random Forest | RandomizedSearchCV, 8/24 | `n_estimators={120,240}`; `max_depth={None,16}`; `min_samples_leaf={1,3}`; `class_weight={None,balanced,balanced_subsample}`; fixed `random_state=42` |\n| HistGradientBoosting | RandomizedSearchCV, 8/64 | `learning_rate={0.05,0.1}`; `max_iter={150,250}`; `max_leaf_nodes={15,31}`; `min_samples_leaf={20,40}`; `l2_regularization={0.0,1.0}`; `class_weight={None,balanced}`; fixed `random_state=42` |\n| Dummy prior | No search | `strategy=prior` |\n\nThe exact frozen configuration carried into final training is recorded for the selected model below.\n\nFor non-selected families, the study's versioned evidence exposes the complete search contract and family-level CV results rather than a final production configuration.\n\n### Feature-policy comparison\n\nThe two shortlisted families are evaluated using their frozen family-search parameters under three feature policies:\n\n- all 16 features;\n- all features except `ShapeFactor2`;\n- seven features after removing the nine confirmed derived dependencies.\n\nValidation results:\n\n| Frozen candidate | Features | Macro F1 | Balanced Accuracy | Worst per-class recall | Log Loss ↓ |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| HistGradientBoosting — all features | 16 | **0.937881** | **0.939131** | **0.870886** | 0.225172 |\n| HistGradientBoosting — without ShapeFactor2 | 15 | 0.936677 | 0.938146 | 0.863291 | 0.229580 |\n| Logistic Regression — all features | 16 | 0.928973 | 0.930908 | 0.863291 | **0.222511** |\n| Logistic Regression — without ShapeFactor2 | 15 | 0.928917 | 0.930814 | 0.860759 | 0.223445 |\n| HistGradientBoosting — without confirmed derived | 7 | 0.919956 | 0.920909 | 0.858586 | 0.278823 |\n| Logistic Regression — without confirmed derived | 7 | 0.915406 | 0.914790 | 0.838384 | 0.271961 |\n\nThe Dummy validation Macro F1 is `0.059052`.\n\n![Validation model comparison](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/FabioAguiar/dataset-study-dry-bean/e3e697c1b60f7b11f4ccb7e82447a6680bc2bfaf/docs/images/model_validation_comparison.png)\n\n### Practical tie and final selection\n\nThe two HistGradientBoosting feature-policy candidates fall inside the predefined practical-tie tolerance.\n\nTheir validation Macro F1 values are:\n\n```text\nall_features                 0.937881\nwithout_shape_factor_2       0.936677\nabsolute difference          0.001204\npractical-tie tolerance      0.002000\n```\n\nBecause the difference is smaller than the predefined `0.002` threshold, Macro F1 alone does not distinguish them materially under the study's selection rule.\n\nThe first applicable predefined tie-break evidence favors `all_features` through validation Balanced Accuracy:\n\n```text\nall_features                 0.939131\nwithout_shape_factor_2       0.938146\n```\n\nThe selected candidate is therefore:\n\n```text\nhist_gradient_boosting__all_features\n```\n\nThe decision is not based on the final-test partition.\n\n### Selected configuration\n\n| Hyperparameter | Selected value |\n|---|---:|\n| `class_weight` | `None` |\n| `learning_rate` | 0.05 |\n| `max_iter` | 250 |\n| `max_leaf_nodes` | 15 |\n| `min_samples_leaf` | 40 |\n| `l2_regularization` | 0.0 |\n| `random_state` | 42 |\n\nAll 16 numerical features are retained. No resampling is used.\n\n## Feature-retention evidence\n\nRemoving `ShapeFactor2` from the selected family changes validation Macro F1 by approximately:\n\n```text\n-0.001204\n```\n\nThis difference falls inside the predefined practical-tie tolerance.\n\nThe selection of `all_features` is resolved by the predefined validation tie-break evidence rather than by treating the small Macro-F1 difference as scientifically decisive.\n\nThe result measures predictive sensitivity only and does not resolve `ShapeFactor2` provenance.\n\nRemoving the nine confirmed derived features changes validation Macro F1 from approximately `0.937881` to `0.919956`, a delta of about:\n\n```text\n-0.017925\n```\n\nThis evidence supports retaining all 16 features in the selected predictive configuration.\n\n![Feature-policy sensitivity](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/FabioAguiar/dataset-study-dry-bean/e3e697c1b60f7b11f4ccb7e82447a6680bc2bfaf/docs/images/feature_policy_sensitivity.png)\n\n## Final holdout evaluation\n\nAfter every selection decision is frozen, the selected classifier is fitted once on train plus validation and evaluated on the final-test partition as part of the **corrected canonical execution**.\n\n| Metric | Validation | Final test |\n|---|---:|---:|\n| **Macro F1** | 0.937881 | **0.941835** |\n| Balanced Accuracy | 0.939131 | **0.939897** |\n| Macro Recall | 0.939131 | **0.939897** |\n| Weighted F1 | 0.926899 | **0.932187** |\n| Accuracy | 0.927032 | **0.932419** |\n| Log Loss ↓ | 0.225172 | **0.181024** |\n| Minimum per-class recall | 0.870886 | **0.868687** |\n\nThe final test contains 2,042 observations.\n\nWithin the corrected canonical protocol, this partition is procedurally isolated from exploratory analysis and model selection: the split is frozen first, exploration is restricted to the training partition, selection uses training and validation evidence, and the final test is evaluated only after the selected configuration is frozen.\n\nHowever, the same seed-42 final-test partition had previously been evaluated once by a **superseded pre-correction execution**.\n\nConsequently, this partition should be described as the frozen final-test partition of the corrected canonical protocol, but **not as a never-before-seen or never-before-opened holdout**.\n\nMacro F1 changes by approximately `+0.003954` from validation to test, while Balanced Accuracy changes by approximately `+0.000766`.\n\nThese differences are descriptive generalization evidence and are not new selection gates.\n\n`SIRA` has the lowest final-test recall.\n\n![Final test confusion matrix](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/FabioAguiar/dataset-study-dry-bean/e3e697c1b60f7b11f4ccb7e82447a6680bc2bfaf/docs/images/final_test_confusion_matrix.png)\n\nThe two largest mutual confusion pairs are:\n\n| Pair | Final-test mutual errors |\n|---|---:|\n| `DERMASON` ↔ `SIRA` | 58 |\n| `BARBUNYA` ↔ `CALI` | 19 |\n\nThe residual errors are therefore concentrated more strongly among some morphologically overlapping varieties rather than being distributed uniformly.\n\n![Validation vs test per-class recall](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/FabioAguiar/dataset-study-dry-bean/e3e697c1b60f7b11f4ccb7e82447a6680bc2bfaf/docs/images/validation_vs_test_per_class_recall.png)\n\n![Validation vs test metrics](https://raw.githubusercontent.com/FabioAguiar/dataset-study-dry-bean/e3e697c1b60f7b11f4ccb7e82447a6680bc2bfaf/docs/images/validation_vs_test_metrics.png)\n\n### Repeated-profile sensitivity\n\nFifteen final-test observations have a complete feature profile also observed in train plus validation.\n\nExcluding those rows leaves 2,027 sensitivity observations:\n\n| Evaluation | Macro F1 |\n|---|---:|\n| Official final test | 0.941835 |\n| Sensitivity subset | 0.941523 |\n| Delta | -0.000312 |\n\nThe official evaluation remains unchanged.\n\nThis analysis does not establish duplicate identity or leakage. Without source observation identifiers, feature-profile equality cannot establish that two records correspond to the same physical grain.\n\n## Understanding the main evaluation metrics\n\n### Macro F1 — 0.941835\n\nMacro F1 computes an F1 score independently for each class and then averages the seven class-level scores with equal weight.\n\nThis is appropriate because the class distribution is unequal and no variety should dominate the evaluation merely because it contains more observations.\n\n**Higher values are better.**\n\n### Balanced Accuracy — 0.939897\n\nBalanced Accuracy averages recall across the seven classes.\n\nIt provides a direct view of whether the classifier maintains useful sensitivity across both frequent and infrequent classes.\n\n**Higher values are better.**\n\n### Accuracy — 0.932419\n\nAccuracy is the overall proportion of correctly classified final-test observations.\n\nBecause each observation contributes equally, larger classes have more influence on this score than smaller classes.\n\nIt should therefore be interpreted together with Macro F1 and Balanced Accuracy.\n\n**Higher values are better.**\n\n### Log Loss — 0.181024\n\nMulticlass Log Loss evaluates the entire predicted probability distribution rather than only the final `argmax` class.\n\nIt penalizes confident probability assignments to incorrect classes and complements label-based metrics.\n\n**Lower values are better.**\n\n## Multiclass prediction and result interpretation\n\nFor each bean sample, the classifier produces a probability distribution across all seven classes.\n\nThe Scientific Study decision rule is:\n\n```text\nargmax\n```\n\nThe class with the largest predicted probability is returned.\n\nFor example:\n\n```text\nSEKER      0.02\nBARBUNYA   0.03\nBOMBAY     0.01\nCALI       0.86\nDERMASON   0.02\nHOROZ      0.04\nSIRA       0.02\n```\n\nproduces:\n\n```text\nCALI\n```\n\nNo positive class or binary threshold is involved.\n\nThe full probability vector is more informative than the predicted class alone. A concentrated distribution indicates that one class is strongly preferred by the fitted model; similar probabilities among several classes indicate greater predictive ambiguity.\n\nProbabilities are explicitly aligned to the official output class order:\n\n```text\nSEKER\nBARBUNYA\nBOMBAY\nCALI\nDERMASON\nHOROZ\nSIRA\n```\n\nThese probabilities describe model support within the observed feature space. They are not guarantees of biological identity.\n\nThe interactive Atlas inference interface uses the separate **Atlas Native Release** described earlier. Its runtime predictions should therefore not be treated as direct executions of the pinned Scientific Study model solely because they use the same dataset schema and class labels.\n\n## What this study demonstrates\n\nDry Bean provides a technically rich example of tabular multiclass classification using numerical measurements derived from images.\n\nIt demonstrates:\n\n- exploratory analysis of multiclass structure using training-only evidence after the split is frozen;\n- unequal class support;\n- deterministic data preparation;\n- a two-stage stratified train, validation, and final-test split;\n- comparison of linear, tree, bagging, and gradient-boosting families;\n- explicit hyperparameter-search spaces;\n- a prior-only baseline;\n- training-only cross-validation;\n- controlled feature-retention experiments;\n- an explicit practical-tie rule and deterministic tie-break process;\n- multiclass Macro F1 and Balanced Accuracy;\n- per-class diagnostics;\n- final evaluation after development decisions are frozen;\n- explicit disclosure of historical final-test exposure from a superseded execution;\n- probability-distribution output;\n- explicit `argmax` class selection;\n- residual confusion among morphologically overlapping classes;\n- separation of predictive evidence from causal interpretation;\n- independent reproduction of the pinned scientific protocol by Atlas.\n\nThe study also illustrates why multiclass quality should not be summarized by accuracy alone.\n\nMacro F1, Balanced Accuracy, per-class recall, and Log Loss provide complementary views of the classifier's behavior.\n\n## Limitations\n\n- The evaluation uses a stratified random snapshot rather than temporal, prospective, or external validation.\n- The seed-42 final-test partition was also evaluated once by a superseded pre-correction execution. The corrected canonical protocol re-derived development decisions from training-only and validation evidence, but the partition cannot be described as never previously opened.\n- External representativeness is not established.\n- Numerical image-derived measurements are evaluated, not the complete image-acquisition and feature-extraction process.\n- Inference-time availability and stability of all 16 measurements are unconfirmed.\n- `ShapeFactor2` source provenance remains unresolved at the configured numerical tolerance.\n- Exact feature-profile equality cannot establish duplicate physical grains without source identifiers.\n- Class probabilities are not established as universally calibrated measures of biological certainty.\n- Morphological overlap remains between several classes.\n- Distribution shift across equipment, environments, cultivars, populations, or acquisition protocols has not been evaluated.\n- No production monitoring or drift policy is established.\n- No application-specific cost model is included.\n- Exploratory feature relationships do not establish causal or agronomic effects.\n- Operational or industrial suitability is not established.\n- The Scientific Study and Atlas Native Training lineages use different experimental protocols, so their metrics should not be interpreted as directly comparable measurements of the same experiment.\n\n## Responsible interpretation\n\nThe evidence supports a clear predictive conclusion:\n\n> Within this dataset and frozen evaluation protocol, numerical measurements of bean size and shape provide strong predictive information for distinguishing seven dry bean varieties. HistGradientBoostingClassifier using all 16 features achieves a final-test Macro F1 of approximately **0.9418** and Balanced Accuracy of approximately **0.9399**.\n\nFour trainable model families are compared through training-only cross-validation.\n\nHistGradientBoostingClassifier and LogisticRegression form the shortlist.\n\nControlled feature-policy evaluation then identifies a practical tie between the two strongest HistGradientBoosting feature policies. The predefined validation tie-break evidence favors `all_features`, after which the final configuration is frozen before final-test evaluation.\n\nThe corrected canonical execution preserves the intended development boundary by restricting exploratory analysis to training data and excluding the final-test partition from model selection.\n\nThe historical exposure of that same seed-42 test partition in a superseded execution is explicitly retained as a limitation and prevents interpreting the final test as a completely untouched external holdout.\n\nAtlas independently reproduced the pinned Scientific Study protocol with status `reproduced_within_tolerance`.\n\nThat reproduction supports reproducibility of the documented scientific result, but it remains distinct from Atlas Native Training.\n\nThe active Atlas Native release powers the interactive inference experience under a different training lineage and experimental protocol. Its metrics should therefore be interpreted separately rather than compared directly with the Scientific Study results.\n\nOverall, the results demonstrate effective classification within the observed dataset and frozen scientific protocol.\n\nThey do not establish causal morphology-to-variety relationships or guarantee the same performance for beans measured under different populations, equipment, environments, or acquisition procedures.\n\nUse beyond this analytical setting would require representative external validation, verification of the complete measurement pipeline, assessment of probability behavior, evaluation under distribution shift, monitoring policies, and application-specific error costs."
    }
  },
  "source_draft_schema_version": "1.0.0"
}